From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: spi: sun6i: Add SPI target (slave) mode

The Allwinner SPI controller works as a host or as a target, selected by
GCR.MODE (H616 user manual v1.0, 9.3.3.5 and 9.3.6.1): with MODE cleared
it waits for the external host to assert SS and drive SPI_CLK, shifts the
TX FIFO out on MISO and fills the RX FIFO from MOSI. The driver only
supported host mode.

Register the controller as an SPI target when its node carries the
standard "spi-slave" property, as spi-imx does. A target transfer is PIO:
the TX FIFO is preloaded (zeros for an RX-only transfer) and refilled from
the TX empty-request interrupt, the RX ready level is set to the rest of
the transfer (or 3/4 of the FIFO), and the transfer completes once
tfr->len bytes have been received, which in a full-duplex shift is also
when the last TX byte has gone out. There are no burst counters in target
mode, so the host sizes the transfer: bytes beyond len stay in the FIFO
and go with the FIFO reset after the transfer, and a FIFO under- or
overrun ends the transfer with -EIO. The wait is interruptible and the
target_abort callback completes it (spidev release, "(null)" written to
the sysfs slave attribute). SS is an input, so set_cs only selects the SSx
input. The module clock is raised to four times the handler's
spi-max-frequency, the host clock to expect, within the 200 MHz the host
path uses, so the input shift register samples reliably. SDM/SDC sample
modes, DMA and the dual/quad modes are host-only and stay off for a
target.

Host mode is unchanged: same mode bits, DMA, clock programming and
interrupt handling; the target paths only run for a controller that was
allocated as a target.

Usage: "spi-slave;" in the controller node, then
echo spidev > /sys/class/spi_slave/spiN/slave (or spi-slave-time,
spi-slave-system-control) for the protocol handler.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---

